00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:10.690
took her to England after her mother's death and on the way over he met a young woman on the boat

00:24:10.690 --> 00:24:17.930
and married her in London. Oh a stepmother and did Aunt Allison recall her name? I gather she

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:23.160
walked out on him quite soon that's why Gwenda was sent to New Zealand but Aunt Allison did

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:29.170
remember one thing they had a house in Devonshire Dartmouth she thought it was but it could have

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:29.850
in Dylmouth.

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:31.490
So that all fits.

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:35.840
And did Gwenda explain to Aunt Alison why she was making all these inquiries?

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:39.610
She said something about having a strong feeling she'd been in England before,

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:41.530
but the line wasn't too hot.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:45.600
And Aunt Alison seems to have left one or two things unsaid in the past to Gwenda.

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:50.090
Obviously it wasn't a happy marriage. I suppose she thought it best forgotten.

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:52.960
There you are, my dear.

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.690
Good morning, Miss Marple. Raymond's battering his typewriter.

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:57.670
Where's Joan?

00:24:57.970 --> 00:24:58.510
In the studio.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.010
Has Giles told you all the news and how brilliant you are?

00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:06.110
We just couldn't get to sleep.

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:10.140
It seemed to explain so much, and then so much it didn't.

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:11.380
Oh, yes, that's true now.

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:13.190
We've got to find out, of course.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:14.860
Find out what?

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:15.970
The truth.

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:17.960
Who was Helen?

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:19.930
And was she really murdered?

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:21.840
I've got to know.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:24.160
Oh, my dear, I do advise you.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.460
I do advise you most strongly not to do that.

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:28.760
Why?

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:29.980
I...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.380
I expect it's all imagination, but Gwenda won't be happy until she knows.

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:38.400
Charles is right, Miss Marple. You've got to see it through. Don't you understand?

00:25:38.860 --> 00:25:41.780
Yes, I understand, because you're both so young and charming,

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:46.330
and you're just beginning, and you simply can't imagine anything.

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:52.500
Yes, I was thinking of Freddie Loveless. He was daryman at Home Farm,

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:57.320
and his mother had apparently once won first prize for chrysanthemums.

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:59.440
Oh, long before Freddie was born.

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:01.240
Quite a triumph for her.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:05.020
And when she died, he tried to find the certificate to have it framed.

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:07.770
He was so proud of her, you see.

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:09.840
It was a grave mistake.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:12.920
Why? Turned out she hadn't won?

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.480
Oh, no, my dear. Turned out she wasn't his mother.

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:23.960
All I'm saying is that I wish, oh, I do so wish, that you'd leave the past alone.

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:25.860
Was it here?

00:26:26.670 --> 00:26:27.870
A bit near the stairs.

00:26:34.990 --> 00:26:39.050
I think it must be true, what Miss Marple said I mean.

00:26:39.470 --> 00:26:42.090
And you can't remember anything else here in the hall.

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:44.260
Just Helen.

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:47.340
Well, no one in Dilmouth seems to know anything,

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:48.660
so it can't have been a murder.

00:26:49.510 --> 00:26:51.990
A parish registry of deaths wasn't much hope.

00:26:54.520 --> 00:26:55.630
What is it?

00:26:56.900 --> 00:26:58.200
You know who we should ask?

00:26:59.130 --> 00:26:59.470
Who?

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:01.460
The land agents.

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:04.860
My father must have bought this house, or at least rented it.

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:06.340
Of course.

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:08.560
Galbraith and Penderly.

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:19.830
It was a holiday at my prep school in Yorkshire, 70 years ago.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:24.700
Good God, I come to me.

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:28.600
I'm an old man, retired years ago.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:32.560
We thought perhaps you might remember, Mr. Galbraith,

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:34.760
because my father came here from India.

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.410
And Mr. Pendley said you were out there in the First World War.

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:39.980
India, do you say?

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:44.580
We think Gwenda's father might have rented Hillside.

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.730
Well, is it called Hillside, then?

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:51.340
Was it, chap?

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:54.100
Young wife and a baby.

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:56.090
Little girl.

00:27:56.410 --> 00:27:57.130
That was me.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:59.970
You don't say so.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:08.140
Well, well, what was his name?

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:09.420
Halliday.

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:12.350
That's right, my dear. Nice fellow.

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:14.380
Very pretty wife.

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:16.920
Young, fair-haired.

00:28:17.780 --> 00:28:20.040
Wanted to be near her people or something like that.

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:23.550
Yes, very pretty.

00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:25.290
Who were her people?

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:29.920
No idea. No idea.

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:59.920
didn't look like you no well St. Catharines yes that's what the place was called it took St. Catharines at eight guineas while Mrs. Findersen was out in Egypt didn't she run off with someone that young girl Halliday's wife we thought you might tell us oh I don't idea

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:02.000
as a devil of a long time ago anyway.

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:05.380
You can tell Beryl I want my drink.

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:27.170
I didn't dream it then.

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:34.990
So, the stepmother was young, and she had fear here.

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:39.330
We still don't know her name.

00:29:41.620 --> 00:29:43.900
We didn't. This is from Raymond.

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:47.280
I asked him to make a check form at Somerset House.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:48.820
What's Somerset House?

00:29:48.940 --> 00:29:51.220
The Registry of Births, Deaths and Marriages.

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:55.400
Yes, here it is. A duplicate of your father's certificate.

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:59.040
Kensington Registry Office, August 7th.

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:07.480
Helvin James Halliday to Helen Spenlove Kennedy.

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:10.450
But it can't have been her you saw.

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:13.210
Galbraith said she went away.

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:16.590
How do we know?

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:19.960
We know she went away.

00:30:56.490 --> 00:30:58.290
Yeah, quite a surprise to you, I'm sure.

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:06.220
My doctor ordered me to the seaside for a change and you always told me how attractive doing this was.

00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:11.880
And I have a friend who had a cook who now takes boarders on the front, so I came here.

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:13.720
Well, why didn't you come and see us?

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:17.060
Oh, I think the old person is rather a nuisance to a young married couple.

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:18.360
No, not you.

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:20.400
Well, it made me welcome, I'm sure.

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:22.710
Well, now, how are you both getting on?

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:25.640
To tell you the truth, I was thinking of you only a moment ago.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:26.640
You were?

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:27.220
Yes.

00:31:27.530 --> 00:31:29.330
I've been studying the local newspaper.

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:31.540
All the source of information.

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:35.150
Ah, then you've seen our advertisement.

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:38.110
I'm afraid we haven't altogether taken your advice.

00:31:38.590 --> 00:31:40.930
Well, good advice is almost certain to be ignored,

00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:43.290
but that's no reason for not giving it.

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.380
Now, I always find it personal so intriguing.

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:49.570
Well, anyone with a knowledge of Helen Spendler,

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:52.660
Helliday, Nea Kennedy, et cetera, et cetera.

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:54.580
So it was Helen.

00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:57.430
Yes, yes it was.

00:31:57.730 --> 00:31:59.970
Well, you must forgive me if I seem concerned, but...

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:01.520
My life has so pure excitement.

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:04.040
And I know you won't think being positive

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:06.460
if I asked you to let me know how you're getting on.

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:07.660
No, of course not.

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:10.300
If it wasn't for you, I'd probably been a loony bin.

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:12.160
No, we'll tell you everything.

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:14.940
And you must come to afternoon tea and see the house.

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:18.320
We've put the advertisement in the Times as well,

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:20.910
so we're bound to get some answers, don't you think?

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:23.590
Yes, I think so.

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:25.130
I do think so, my dear.

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:29.250
Gould's Hill, Woodley Bolton.

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:33.610
Dear son, in answer to your advertisement in the Times,

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:36.210
Helen Spenlove Kennedy is my sister.

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:39.180
I've lost touch with her for many years.

00:32:39.400 --> 00:32:41.000
We should be glad to have news of her.

00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:44.320
Yours faithfully, James Kennedy, MD.

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:48.720
Oh, you don't mean to tell me you're Gwenny.

00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:51.720
Yes, I'm Gwenny.

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:53.700
This goes for some ceremony.

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:54.580
We must have tea.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:55.580
Please sit down.

00:32:55.900 --> 00:32:56.200
Thank you.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:32:59.840
So.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:02.370
You've bought the house in Dylmouth, have you?

00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:05.000
Yes. It's quite a strange coincidence.

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:06.600
We bought Hillside.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:08.690
I believe you told me on the phone.

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:11.500
It was for sale, you see.

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:15.480
It's the same house my father had all those years ago.

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:18.430
Hillside? Oh, but surely...

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:20.950
They changed the name, I think, just before the war.

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:22.510
I got the right place.

00:33:22.890 --> 00:33:25.050
On the Leehampton Road, as you go down into the town.

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:27.830
Yes. It used to be called St. Catherine's.

00:33:28.270 --> 00:33:29.060
Of course.

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:36.870
Ah, Mrs Rowe, we thought we'd have some tea, some cakes or some hot butter toast or something.

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:42.200
I don't usually go in for tea.

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:47.490
What made you want to come back to St Catherine's? You can't remember much about it, surely.

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:51.040
No, very little. But somehow it felt like home.

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:52.450
It felt like home.

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:58.860
So I hoped you could tell me all about it, my father and Helen.

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:01.120
Well, everything.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:02.960
Well, there's not much.

00:34:04.330 --> 00:34:05.630
Helen was coming back from India.

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:07.110
She met your father on the boat.

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:09.810
He'd lost his wife, fell in love with her,

00:34:10.370 --> 00:34:11.930
and she felt sorry for him.

00:34:12.310 --> 00:34:12.970
And there you are.

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:16.210
It's hard to know how these things happen.

00:34:17.380 --> 00:34:20.440
But they married in London, and then they came down to me.

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.140
I was in practice in Dilmuth then.

00:34:23.490 --> 00:34:24.670
A nice fellow, Kelvin.

00:34:24.950 --> 00:34:28.420
A bit nervy and run down, but they were very happy together then.

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:29.980
However...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:32.920
In less than a year, she'd run away with someone else.

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:34.360
You probably know about that.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:36.340
Who did she run away with?

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:39.120
She didn't tell me. I wasn't in her confidence.

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:42.320
But I'd seen, I couldn't help seeing,

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:44.940
there was some friction between her and Kelvin.

00:34:46.230 --> 00:34:48.170
She didn't want me to know what was going on.

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:49.250
I wouldn't have approved.

00:34:50.720 --> 00:34:52.260
They often had people to stay with them.

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:53.580
I always imagined it was one of them.

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:55.820
There wasn't a divorce then?

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:57.860
Oh, no. Ellen didn't want a divorce.

00:34:58.450 --> 00:34:59.710
Kelvin told me that.

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:03.560
That's why I imagine, perhaps wrongly, it was a case of some married man.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:06.340
Someone whose wife was a Catholic, perhaps.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:07.480
And my father?

00:35:08.050 --> 00:35:09.350
He didn't want a divorce either.

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:14.350
Why did he suddenly decide to send me to New Zealand?

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:17.600
Well, I gather your people out there were pressing him,

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:19.400
and after the breakup of the marriage...

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:20.820
But why didn't he take me himself?

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:22.220
I don't know.

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:26.250
He was in rather poor health at the time.

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:27.690
But what did he die of?

00:35:28.590 --> 00:35:29.970
I'm afraid I don't remember all the...

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.510
details as i say he was in poor health and he went to a sanatorium somewhere on the east coast

00:35:35.510 --> 00:35:44.180
and he died there about two years later where was it exactly i'm sorry i don't remember can you

00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:50.820
tell us where he was buried you know i don't think we should dwell on the past it was all such a long

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:57.820
time ago and we rather lost touch when he left dilmouth i've no idea at all where your father

00:35:57.820 --> 00:35:59.880
is buried that's strange

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:02.160
Well, not really. Helen was the link.

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:06.200
I was always very fond of Helen, tried to take care of her.

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:10.060
She was my half-sister, you know, much younger than I was.

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:14.220
She got involved early on with the most unsuitable young man,

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:16.540
and I took care of that.

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:20.160
And then she elected to go to India to marry Walter Fane,

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:22.580
son of Dilmuth's leading solicitor,

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:24.680
and frankly, as dull as dishwater.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:28.640
When she reached Bombay and saw him again, it was all off.

00:36:29.420 --> 00:36:29.980
And then...

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:32.850
Well, she met your father coming home.

00:36:33.970 --> 00:36:37.590
You see what I mean? I was always worried about Helen.

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:42.070
Do you know where she is at all? I'd like to get in touch with her again.

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:47.120
No, no, we don't know at all.

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:50.510
That's one of the reasons we came to see you.

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:55.290
But I thought from your advertisement.

00:36:57.370 --> 00:36:59.210
So tell me, why did you advertise?

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:08.820
We were hoping. We thought someone who knew her might be able to tell me something about my father.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:14.730
Well, I'm sorry. That's all I know.

00:37:21.630 --> 00:37:22.350
What did you make of it?

00:37:22.450 --> 00:37:27.830
I think it's hiding something. I wish we'd never started this.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:46.460
I almost strained the chips onto it.

00:37:46.970 --> 00:37:50.470
Will anyone with a knowledge of Ellen Spenlove Allarday,

00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:54.260
nae Kennedy, communicate with Mrs. Newman...

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.500
Oh, that's Mrs. Allarday I was in service with at Dilma.

00:37:58.170 --> 00:37:59.590
Her name was Ellen, right enough.

00:38:00.250 --> 00:38:04.190
And she was sister to Dr. Kennedy and has cured my chillblings.

00:38:04.490 --> 00:38:06.590
You think there's any money in it?

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:08.470
Might be a will or something.

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:10.030
It's a powerful long time ago.

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:13.910
You don't think it could be police, do you, Jim?

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:15.420
Whatever.

00:38:16.290 --> 00:38:18.350
You know what I always thought.

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:21.470
And I said it to Edie, but she wouldn't have it.

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:23.650
And I'd have said a tiny bit more at the time.

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:27.930
Only I nipped out the pictures that night and I didn't want to draw attention to it.

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:29.970
Now, it wouldn't likely be...

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:36.430
police not after all this time but it doesn't say to your advantage do you

00:38:36.430 --> 00:38:45.080
think I should Jim not if I was you Lily never not only leads to trouble that

00:38:45.080 --> 00:38:52.230
sort of thing when supper ready

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:25.220
hello mr reed it's dr kennedy here hello dr kennedy

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:41.990
Seems odd to be healed again.

00:39:44.420 --> 00:39:49.250
Well now, I take it you're still determined to find the sanatorium where your father died.

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:50.590
Yes, yes we are.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:53.450
It should be easy enough for you, I imagine, after what I've told you.

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:55.000
Shall we sit down?

00:39:55.490 --> 00:39:59.920
Thank you. So, I thought it better you heard the fact.

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:20.340
for me. Won't do you or anybody any good, Gwenny. There it is. Your father wasn't physically

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:29.920
ill. The sanatorium in question was a mental home. A mental home? You mean he was mad?

00:40:31.240 --> 00:40:33.260
He had a very severe mental breakdown.

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:37.190
He was suffering from delusional obsessions.

00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:39.370
What kind of delusions?

00:40:40.540 --> 00:40:43.750
He was under the impression that he'd strangled his wife.

00:40:44.270 --> 00:40:46.920
Not strangled.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:49.720
And had he?

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:53.080
Of course he hadn't.

00:40:54.020 --> 00:40:54.580
How do you know?

00:40:55.660 --> 00:40:57.900
Dear girl, there was never any question of such a thing.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:40:59.960
He'd been in a very...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.480
very unbalanced state for some time and the shock when Helen left him sent him

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:11.770
over the edge if a man would rather his wife was dead than unfaithful he can

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:17.870
persuade himself of anything even that he killed her so you're quite sure there's

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:22.580
no question that he actually did what he said oh quite sure I'd had two

00:41:22.580 --> 00:41:28.270
letters from Helen one when she went away after a week to France and another

00:41:28.270 --> 00:41:29.250
about six months later

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:32.780
No, the whole thing was a delusion, pure and simple.

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:36.140
Can you tell us about it?

00:41:36.520 --> 00:41:37.300
I tried to.

00:41:40.110 --> 00:41:41.550
He'd been having dreams.

00:41:42.490 --> 00:41:45.770
And the dreams always ended up in the same way, with throttling Helen.

